The three clauses of C2.5 are assessed against the design literature of market socialism (a comprehensive system). The core theoretical sources — Lange & Lerner (1930s), Yugoslav practice (1950s–80s), Roemer’s coupon socialism (1994), Schweickart’s Economic Democracy (2002), and related scholarship — consistently describe a system in which (1) workers may freely leave a firm or cooperative and join another or start a new one with access to social capital, while essential benefits (healthcare, education, basic income/pension) are universal and not tied to a specific employer; (2) non-participants (unemployed, retirees, those outside the labor force) receive the same essential benefits as participants, with no differential treatment in the social wage; (3) geographic mobility within the nation is unrestricted and benefits are portable, while international emigration is treated as a political right. No structural mechanism in the design penalizes exit or makes coherence depend on near-universal participation. The 3‑month benchmark for feasible exit is met for job/cooperative changes; emigration timing is a state administrative matter, not an economic penalty imposed by the system.

All three clauses are cleared on the basis of the system’s own design principles.

